            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The case concerns whether the Texas death\u2011penalty sentencing process considered mitigating evidence about the defendant\u2019s childhood abuse and mental impairment, as required by the Supreme Court\u2019s decisions in Penry v. Lynaugh, Graham, and Johnson."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Robert C. Owen",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 8,
              "start": 8
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Robert C. Owen, petitioner argues that jurors were not asked whether mitigating evidence reduced culpability enough to warrant a life sentence, making the death sentences unreliable."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Robert C. Owen",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Robert C. Owen, the Fifth Circuit\u2019s settled approach requires that jurors have a meaningful basis to consider mitigating evidence, a standard the lower courts allegedly failed to meet."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Robert C. Owen",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 4,
                "start": 4
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Robert C. Owen, the petitioners contend that the lower courts failed to give jurors a meaningful basis to consider mitigating evidence of childhood abuse and mental impairment, contrary to Fifth Circuit precedent."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Robert C. Owen",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0000-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 8,
                "start": 8
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Robert C. Owen, petitioners argue that jurors were never asked whether the mitigating evidence reduced the defendant\u2019s culpability enough to warrant a life sentence, rendering the death sentences unreliable."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Edward L. Marshall",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0001-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 21,
                "start": 21
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Edward L. Marshall, citing Graham and Johnson, petitioners note that the Eighth Amendment requires only that jurors be able to consider mitigating evidence in some manner, not in every conceivable way."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Marshall",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 38,
                "start": 38
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Marshall, petitioners assert there was a reasonable likelihood that jurors were precluded from considering the relevant mitigating evidence."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Owen",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3f2667a0ebbe1e16629abc1f9462fe1fb345321df7707293a52f8e4bfb271a98",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3f2667a0ebbe-0002-0007"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 44,
                "start": 44
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Owen, petitioners remind the Court that Graham was a Teague case, underscoring its precedential weight."
        }
      ],
